---
export interface Props {
  title?: string;
  noindex?: boolean;
  /**
   * This page's own path, e.g. `/dates`. A page that does not name itself in `rel=canonical` is telling
   * every crawler it is a copy of the homepage, which is exactly how a new page fails to get indexed;
   * `chat.astro`, `dates.astro` and `join.astro` now pass theirs (`dates.astro` under the route D373 moved).
   * Unset means the apex, which is the homepage.
   */
  path?: string;
}

const { title = seo.title, noindex = false, path = '' } = Astro.props;
const canonical = seo.canonical.replace(/\/$/, '') + path;
const robots = noindex ? 'noindex,nofollow' : 'index,follow';
// Round-6 review (2026-11-16), Site-wide/Add: "page titles with the category and the city". Only the home page's
// title said what the site is, and every other page's title was its own name alone (D396). The share card and a
// search result are the surfaces where that phrase matters most, so `og:title` and `twitter:title` now mirror the
// PAGE's title rather than a single site-wide one - and `title` defaults to `seo.title`, so a page that passes no
// title still gets the site's descriptor. `scripts/check-copy.mjs`'s `meta-mirror-bound` rule was changed in the
// same commit that made this the shape: it now asserts the mirror is bound to the page title AND that the page
// title defaults to `seo.title`, which is the pair of facts that keeps a page from shipping a card with no
// descriptor on it.
// The ratified serif pair, latin subset only (design/decisions.json typeFamily: 33,116 B + 29,588 B).
// `import "@fontsource/pt-serif"` pulled every subset — the build was shipping cyrillic woff2 for a
// site whose copy is English.
import "@fontsource/pt-serif/latin-400.css";
import "@fontsource/pt-serif/latin-700.css";
import "../styles/fonts.css";
import "../styles/global.css";
import { seo } from "../config.json";
import Navigation from '../components/Navigation.astro';
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en-US">
  <head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width" />
    <link rel="icon" type="image/svg+xml" href="/favicon.svg" />
    <link rel="canonical" href={canonical} />
    <meta name="robots" content={robots} />
    <meta name="googlebot" content={robots} />
    <meta name="description" content={seo.description} />
    <meta property="og:url" content={canonical} />
    <meta property="og:title" content={title} />
    <meta property="og:description" content={seo.description} />
    <meta property="og:type" content="website" />
    <meta property="og:image" content={seo.canonical + seo.image} />
    <meta property="og:image:width" content="1200" />
    <meta property="og:image:height" content="630" />
    <meta property="og:site_name" content={seo.name} />
    <meta property="og:locale" content={seo.locale} />
    <meta name="twitter:card" content="summary_large_image" />
    <meta name="twitter:title" content={title} />
    <meta name="twitter:description" content={seo.description} />

    <title>{title}</title>

  </head>
  <body class="bg-paper text-ink">
    <!-- No sprite here. The glyph data behind every sign-up mark (S026, D294) is printed by
         src/components/SignUpLinks.astro in front of the first cluster on the page, so a page that paints no
         mark ships no glyph bytes: inserting the sprite here would cost +2,201 gzip @measured 2026-11-13 by
         `node design/mark-cost.mjs` - 41.3% of /chat's 5,327 gzip bytes - for drawings that page never
         paints. src/components/PlatformMarks.astro holds the arithmetic that decides sprite vs inline, and
         scripts/check-sprite-claim.mjs is the gate that keeps the sprite out of this shell (D297). -->
    <!-- The skip link. A keyboard reader lands on the header on every page and has to cross the mark, three
         labels and the date chip before reaching the page's own words — WCAG 2.4.1 (Bypass Blocks) asks for one
         control that jumps past repeated chrome, and this tree had none: **0 of 76 anchors** on the 6 built pages
         carried a `#` fragment at all @measured 2026-11-21 by `npm run check:cta-context`, which is also why the
         `skip-link` entry in `design/cta-rules.mjs`'s EXEMPTIONS died of `exemption-stale` — an exemption for a
         link that does not exist is a green light for a hole. It is `sr-only` until focused, so it ships no ink:
         Tailwind's `sr-only` clips it to a **1 x 1px box with a transparent fill** at rest @measured 2026-11-23 by
         `node design/grill/dist-measure.mjs dist/client / design/grill/render-measure.js 1240 1400` — which is why
         it costs no geometry: the nav row on `/` needs **283 of 1164 / 580 / 346**, and **234 of 276** at the 320
         floor, identically with and without the anchor in the built page @measured 2026-11-23 by
         `node design/render-gate.mjs / --no-build` run twice over one build with the 322-byte anchor deleted from
         `dist/client/index.html` between the runs. That measured 1px box is also what `design/render-gate.mjs`'
         `hiddenAtRest` carve-out keys on: the anchor carries `focus:bg-ink`, so the class test calls it
         control-shaped, and the 48px ladder excuses it only because there is no box at rest to hold to the ladder.
         The counterfactual is `check:render:selftest`'s "skip link painted at rest, no role" case — strip `sr-only`
         from the built page and the same element fails the same rule, so the exemption cannot be claimed by a class
         name.
         On focus it becomes a real control at the top-left, painted with the same ink/paper pair and the same focus
         ring every other control on the site takes (`outline-2 offset-2 outline-accent`), and `min-h-8` (32px) so it
         clears the 24px pointer floor while it is visible. The focused box is the one control geometry on this site
         no browser gate reads: the render gate measures each page at rest.
         `scripts/check-outline.mjs`'s `skip-link`, `skip-link-late` and `skip-link-target` rules are what stop it
         vanishing, arriving after the chrome it skips, or pointing at an id that is not the page's own main
         landmark. Write no angle-bracket tag name inside this shell's comments: the gates read the built page, and
         a `<` in a comment is a byte in the artifact — `check:naming`'s `eyebrow-above-heading` and
         `check:outline`'s `skip-link-target` both went red on 2026-11-23 because a note in this file quoted the
         main landmark's tag name, and the scan found the quote before it found the element. -->
    <a href="#main" class="sr-only focus:not-sr-only focus:absolute focus:top-2 focus:left-2 focus:z-50 focus:inline-flex focus:min-h-8 focus:items-center focus:px-4 focus:py-2 focus:rounded-control focus:bg-ink focus:text-paper focus:text-label focus:outline-2 focus:outline-offset-2 focus:outline-accent">Skip to content</a>
    <Navigation />
    <slot />
    <style lang="scss" is:global>
      /* `::selection` is the one place this file paints, and the pair is measured: paper on ink =
         17.15 @measured 2026-11-14 by node design/grill/palette-remap.mjs (12.02 before D316 moved the ink
         and the paper). The retired amber pair measured
         2.06, under the 4.5 bar; the amber is delta-OKLab 0.019 from Amazon orange #FF9900 (D82/D88),
         which is why the token went, not just this rule. The amber also used to sit in
         src/config.json as `theme.color`, still declaring a colour nothing read — that key is gone
         too, so the retired hue has one home left in this repo (see the social-share note in
         .notes/NEXT.md). */
      ::selection {
        background: var(--color-ink);
        color: var(--color-paper);
      }

      /* No keyframes ship in src/. The one that used to sit here pulsed the footer's sum glyph at
         0.5s infinite linear alternate-reverse, which has no tier in the motion grammar (150ms colour,
         250ms movement) — guarded decoration is still decoration, so the operator ruled it out with the
         rest of the old footer band (2026-11-02). `ratify-check` now fails on any `@keyframes` in live
         src, and separately fails on an unguarded `animation:` write, so motion can come back only by
         taking a tier AND its reduced-motion guard. */

      /* The 404 page's link writes the `underline` utility. A GLOBAL class of the same name used to
         sit here: an amber highlighter bar with `text-decoration: none`, later in the cascade than the
         utility, so every "underline" on the site painted amber and was NOT underlined. It is why the
         class gate could not see the last amber write on the site — the appearance came from a <style>
         block, not a class. Keep it that way: no global element/class rules in this file. */
    </style>
  </body>
</html>
